```java
public static int longestStableRun(int[] readings) {
    int start = 0, longest = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < readings.length; i++) {
        if (readings[i] < 0 || readings[i] > 100) {
            start = i + 1;
            continue;
        }
        if (i > start && Math.abs(readings[i] - readings[i - 1]) > 3) start = i;
        longest = Math.max(longest, i - start + 1);
    }
    return longest;
}
```Here start is the beginning of the currently eligible span. An invalid value moves it past that position, so nothing can cross an invalid entry. A gap above three moves start to the later reading, which remains a valid singleton. For every valid position, i-start+1 counts readings rather than gaps, and longest records the largest count seen. It begins at zero, so an empty array and invalid-only input return zero. The scan reads the array without modifying it. Gaps of exactly three do not move start. For [4,8,11], start is 0 at the first value, becomes 1 at 8, and stays 1 at 11, producing lengths 1,1,2.

Use || to reject values below 0 or above 100. In context the implementation is:

public static int longestStableRun(int[] readings) {
    boolean previousValid = false;
    int previous = 0, count = 0, max = 0;
    for (int reading : readings) {
        boolean valid = reading >= 0 && reading <= 100;
        if (!valid) count = 0;
        else if (previousValid && Math.abs(reading - previous) <= 3) count++;
        else count = 1;
        max = Math.max(max, count);
        previous = reading;
        previousValid = valid;
    }
    return max;
}
